FAISS 1.11.0 CPU fallback
========================

Source: https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/releases/tag/v1.11.0
Archive: https://codeload.github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/tar.gz/refs/tags/v1.11.0
SHA256: c5d517da6deb6a6d74290d7145331fc7474426025e2d826fa4a6d40670f4493c

faiss-1.11.0.tar.gz is the unmodified upstream source archive. Its LICENSE and
all file-level notices are retained. Only the generic CPU library is compiled;
Python, CUDA, cuVS, benchmarks and upstream tests are not built. Installation
does not download software or invoke a system package manager.

Makefile.static lists exactly the upstream generic CPU target's sources and
uses the active R toolchain. It omits OnDiskInvertedLists on Windows, as does
upstream CMake. NDEBUG matches the upstream Release build. R's optimization
and debug flags are retained; no machine-specific instruction set is forced.

r-compat.patch is applied only to the extracted package-local build tree:
- Two MinGW error messages use FAISS_THROW_MSG rather than an empty variadic
  FAISS_THROW_FMT invocation; Windows error codes use unsigned-long formats.
- FAISS_STATIC avoids DLL import/export decoration for the static library.
- FAISS assertion failures and two gnuplot-output failures throw FAISS
  exceptions instead of calling abort. Threaded-index cleanup checks the
  worker pointer without throwing from a noexcept destructor.
- Discarded future results are marked explicitly; an unused microbenchmark
  helper is annotated, and Clang is not mistaken for GCC < 8 on ARM.
- Two unused process-global srand calls are removed. FAISS's own local random
  generators and seeds remain unchanged.
- Derived additive-quantizer indexes attach their quantizer only after its
  construction, avoiding an uninitialized read in the base constructor. The
  configure probe checks all four quantizer families after linking.
- Parallel sorting uses OpenMP active-level controls instead of the deprecated
  nested-parallelism API and restores the caller's previous setting.
- Header-only emulated SIMD helpers are inline; MinGW alignment uses standard
  alignas, a spurious leading fallthrough annotation is removed, and NSG index
  deserialization uses exhaustive conditional construction.
- FAISS file I/O includes cerrno explicitly rather than depending on a
  transitive include from a particular C++ standard library.
- The mapped-file owner uses an explicit fclose deleter type, avoiding a GCC
  diagnostic about function attributes used as a template argument.

configure compiles and loads a small OpenMP probe first. If R supplies no
working OpenMP flag, it tests -fopenmp and uses the successful flags consistently
for the bundled library, the linkage probe and the package. It never replaces
the user's compiler or the system OpenMP installation. Failed final probes
print their compiler/linker diagnostics into the installation log.

The upstream CPU source uses the Expat License. NVIDIA-contributed build/GPU
infrastructure in the unmodified source archive also carries Apache-2.0
notices; a complete copy of that license is provided as FAISS-APACHE-LICENSE.
Those GPU/build-system components are not compiled by this CPU fallback.

FAISS console output is buffered by faissr_console.cpp, including messages
from OpenMP workers, and emitted with Rprintf/REprintf on the calling R thread.
Ordinary file writes remain file writes. Diagnostic buffering is bounded to
1 MiB per outer call and reports truncation. Search algorithms are unchanged.

To force this path, use --configure-args='--with-faiss=bundled' or set
FAISSR_FAISS_SOURCE=bundled. The default auto uses suitable installed FAISS
first; system disables the fallback. Windows alone also supports none for an
explicit diagnostic-only test, never as an automatic substitute for FAISS.

The generated build/ directory is removed by cleanup and excluded from the
source package. No precompiled library is shipped in the source archive.
